Add nix support until we could merge the nix package upstream. Also, this change exports a simple nixos module like `recyclarr`(https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/blob/nixos-unstable/nixos/modules/services/misc/recyclarr.nix) so that users can do `services.configarr = { ...... };`

## Testing

I used to have this module in my config, and so I could do 

```
imports = [
  ./module
  ./config.nix
];
```

removing `.module`, then building,

```
error: The option `services.configarr' does not exist. Definition values:
```

I included it in my flake like

```
configarr = {
  inputs = {
    flake-parts.follows = "flake-parts";
    nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
    systems.follows = "systems";
  };
  url = "path:/home/venky/configarr";
};
```

and shifted

```
imports = [
  ./module
  ./config.nix
];
```

to

```
imports = [
  inputs.configarr.nixosModules.default
  ./config.nix
];
```

and everything is working as usual

## Summary by Sourcery

Add Nix support by providing a flake, package derivation, and NixOS module for configuring configarr as a systemd service

New Features:
- Add a NixOS module to configure and schedule the configarr service with systemd
- Provide a Nix derivation to build and install configarr via PNPM in Nix environments
- Introduce flake.nix to expose configarr packages and nixosModules for multiple platforms

Enhancements:
- Define module options in options.nix for customizing service settings
- Consolidate NixOS module imports in default.nix

Documentation:
- Update README with usage instructions for the NixOS module

The `read_parse_program_headers` function was writing program headers far beyond allocated memory due to incorrect array indexing.

## Root Cause

Used byte offset as array index:
```c
int offset_output = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < data->elf_hdr.e_phnum; i++) {
    memcpy(&data->prgm_hdrs[offset_output], &current, sizeof(Elf64_Phdr));
    offset_output += sizeof(Elf64_Phdr);  // 56 bytes
}
```

When indexing `prgm_hdrs[offset_output]`, the compiler calculates address as `prgm_hdrs + (offset_output * sizeof(Elf64_Phdr))`, causing writes at indices [0, 56, 112, 168...] instead of [0, 1, 2, 3...].

## Fix

Replace byte offset with loop index:
```c
for (int i = 0; i < data->elf_hdr.e_phnum; i++) {
    memcpy(&data->prgm_hdrs[i], &current, sizeof(Elf64_Phdr));
}
```

All program headers now correctly populate the array sequentially.

